# Learn more https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/getting-started-with-git/ignoring-files

# dependencies
node_modules/

# Expo
.expo/
dist/
# Maestro e2e reports and per-run artifacts (already under dist/, kept explicit
# so the path is not accidentally un-ignored when dist/ is narrowed).
dist/e2e-reports/
credentials.json
eas.json.tmp
web-build/
expo-env.d.ts

# Native
.kotlin/
*.orig.*
*.jks
*.p8
*.p12
*.key
*.mobileprovision

# Metro
.metro-health-check*

# debug
npm-debug.*
yarn-debug.*
yarn-error.*

# macOS
.DS_Store
*.pem

# local env files
.env*.local

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo

example

# generated native folders
/ios
/android
/.claude/worktrees

# The App Store Connect metadata `eas metadata:push` reads. It carries the
# review contact -- a named individual and their personal mobile number -- so
# an earlier pass untracked it.
#
# Kept tracked by the owner's decision: the contact fields are theirs to
# publish or not, and the file carries the store URLs for eight locales, which
# are worth versioning. `.easignore` still keeps it out of build uploads.
# If that call is ever reversed, the way back is a dynamic `store.config.js`
# reading the contact from the environment -- not a redacted copy of this file,
# which would be pushed to Apple and be wrong.

# The maintainers' card tracker: a private board's api_url, account, board id,
# column ids and user ids. Nothing a reader of this repo can use, and not ours
# to publish. It stays on the machines that have the CLI. NOTE: a fresh `git
# worktree add` will not have it -- copy it in, or `git add -f` this line away
# if that trade stops being worth it.
/.fizzyx.yaml

# Another agent's local settings, ignored here rather than only in the
# maintainers' global gitignore, so a contributor's copy does not get committed
# by accident.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Internal runbooks, kept out of a public repo on their own stated grounds --
# each of them says "nothing in this file is ever pasted anywhere". Store
# submission checklists, the paste runs, the App Review demo-QR reply, the two
# launch manuals and the roadmap. They stay with the maintainers.
/private/

# The store-facing trees, which this repository deliberately does not carry.
#
#   /store/       what `eas metadata:pull` writes back from App Store Connect
#   /docs/store/  the listing copy per locale, the App Review notes, the
#                 submitted screenshots
#   /marketing/   the screenshot-rendering project: its own bun.lock and
#                 tsconfig, its raw captures, backgrounds and rendered decks
#
# Together they were 93MB of a 103MB tree, none of it read by the app, the
# build or the tests -- so they live with the maintainers, next to the runbooks
# above. The three screenshots the README shows were copied into
# `assets/readme/` instead, which is where a front page's images belong.
#
# These rules exist so that copying one of those trees back in does not quietly
# commit it. Bringing one back for real means deleting its line and saying why.
/store/
/docs/store/
/marketing/

# The maintainers own agent skills. Useful to them, noise in a public tree,
# and they name a workflow a contributor does not have to follow. Kept on the
# machines that use them.
/.agents/

# One machine's LaunchAgent: it hardcodes that machine's home directory, its
# tailnet address and its log paths. Useful to its owner, meaningless to anyone
# else, and not ours to publish.
/scripts/dev.osuki.muqun-apk-server.plist

# Scratch renders the maintainers look at while designing. They were committed
# by accident; they are working files, not source.
/design-preview/
